#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# neville_theta_n

#% text
# `neville_theta_n(z, m)` gives the Neville theta function `theta_n(z | m)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `theta_n(z | m)` is `theta_4(pi*z/(2*K(m)), q)/theta_4(0, q)`, with the nome `q` of `m` (DLMF 20.1, 22.2). It is even and `theta_n(0 | m)` is 1.

#% item
# It is the common denominator of the quotients that give `sn`, `cn` and `dn`.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
neville_theta_n(0.5, 0.3)
#% output
#= 1.0182516442144647

#% cell
neville_theta_n(0, 0.3)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
neville_theta_n(1.0, 0.9)
#% output
#= 1.2530036936634286

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The quotient `theta_d/theta_n` is `dn`:

#% cell
neville_theta_d(1.1, 0.6)/neville_theta_n(1.1, 0.6) - jacobi_dn(1.1, 0.6)
#% output
#= 1.1102230246251565e-16

